{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqh1dkl8w1p","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"FU-2 desync root cause is attempt-merge stash pop on the shared main worktree, not an in process save race","summary":"SPEC IS INCOMPLETE: the FU-2 \"overlay memory/disk edge desync\" is NOT an in-process save race. Daemon saves are SYNCHRONOUS (fs.writeFileSync + renameSync, lib/overlay.js:242) so they cannot interleave within one process, and the daemon code contains no `git stash`. Verified real cause: the orchestrator's attempt-merge automation runs `git merge orch/attempt/...` on the MAIN working tree continuously (git reflog shows back-to-back `merge orch/attempt` operations). When that tree carries uncommitted changes, the automation git-stashes them, merges, then git-stash-pops — the pop conflicts and clobbers. Evidence captured live: a `stash@{0}: WIP on main` entry plus `<<<<<<< Updated upstream / >>>>>>> Stashed changes` markers injected into uncommitted source files, and add_dependency/remove_dependency edge mutations that \"did not stick\" because the merge overwrote .graph from the attempt branch. Because .graph is VERSION-CONTROLLED, LIVE-WRITTEN runtime state, committing it and merging each attempt's .graph back into a concurrently-written main checkout is inherently racy. FIX IS ARCHITECTURAL, not an in-process write-queue: run attempt merges in a DEDICATED worktree rather than the main checkout; and/or never stash-pop over unrelated uncommitted work; and/or reconsider tracking live .graph in git. The DEADLOCK half of FU-2 was fixed separately and robustly (firstSeen-anchored hard ceiling, commit 751d58e) — it is immune to this desync by design.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-16T19:29:48.668Z","vec":[-0.040661342442035675,-0.005314493086189032,-0.025769811123609543,0.005984100978821516,0.04001805558800697,-0.09295270591974258,-0.043639812618494034,0.05014738067984581,0.09069539606571198,0.05599427595734596,0.035081177949905396,0.03390955924987793,-0.022048832